WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.360
Since the dawn of time, we have&nbsp;
raised our eyes to the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.720
observing and dreaming of the unknown&nbsp;
worlds that the universe might shelter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.040
Imagine that we could leave Earth,&nbsp;
and travel through space and time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:43.840
in order to explore these inaccessible&nbsp;
places, discovering the Cosmos.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.600
We are one and a half billion&nbsp;
kilometers away from the Sun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:11.840
Seen from here, our star is no more&nbsp;
than a dot, lost in an immensity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.400
In front of us stands Saturn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.480
It is the sixth planet of the solar system.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:40.880
Saturn is a huge ball of gas. Ten times&nbsp;
larger than Earth, the gas giant’s diameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:53.840
measures nearly 120,000 km, for a mass&nbsp;
of 500 000 billion billion tonnes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.600
Being so far from the Sun, it takes&nbsp;
almost 30 years to orbit around it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:21.840
Here, one year amounts to 11,000 days on Earth.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.720
The very thick atmosphere of Saturn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:35.840
is composed mainly of hydrogen, to which is added&nbsp;
some helium, and traces of other rare gases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:45.040
These gases, which are very light, provide&nbsp;
Saturn with a remarkably low average density.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:03:03.840
If it could be submerged in a large enough ocean,&nbsp;
it would float on the surface of the water.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.560
Covered in yellow and orange colors,&nbsp;
Saturn's atmosphere is far from uniform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.000
Its color is due to the chemical&nbsp;
elements that compose it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:33.280
It sometimes presents blue shades resulting from&nbsp;
complex phenomena related to how light scatters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:45.840
These are the same phenomena that give&nbsp;
our sky its blue colour, on Earth.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:04:00.320
On Saturn’s surface, a freezing&nbsp;
temperature of negative 180°C prevails.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:13.920
Gas clouds circulate around the planet,&nbsp;
and form a stack of horizontal stripes.&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.280
In this incessant flow, it sometimes&nbsp;
happens that huge thunderstorms form.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.880
These extremely powerful electric storms&nbsp;
can last for several months and extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:41.840
over thousands of kilometers, giving rise to&nbsp;
raging winds that can reach incredible speeds.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.480
At the North Pole the course of the&nbsp;
winds draws a very strange cloud pattern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.960
Its mysterious, hexagonal-shape has&nbsp;
yet to find a definite explanation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:23.840
In ultraviolet light, we can also&nbsp;
distinguish magnificent auroras.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.480
These intriguing polar lights,&nbsp;
similar to those found on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.840
arise from the interaction of solar&nbsp;
winds with the planet's magnetic field.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.800
Saturn is surrounded by an impressive&nbsp;
system of concentric rings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:05.840
These rings are made up of fine particles of&nbsp;
ice and dust, which revolve around the planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.800
Extremely thin, they are only&nbsp;
a few dozens of meters thick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:19.840
while spanning hundreds of&nbsp;
thousands of kilometers in radius.&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:34.400
The origin of these rings is still a mystery.&nbsp;
They are perhaps the remnants of a moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:42.480
which would have ventured too close to the planet.&nbsp;
Torn and distorted by the attraction of Saturn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.840
it would have eventually exploded,&nbsp;
pulverized by the planet’s tidal forces.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.600
Saturn is outrageously massive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:05.120
Its very strong gravitational influence&nbsp;
allows it to keep in orbit over sixty moons.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:11.840
Some of these moons orbit inside the rings.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:24.560
Among them, for instance, is Pan. Orbiting within&nbsp;
the Encke Gap, its gravitational pull deflects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:34.800
the trajectories of surrounding particles. The&nbsp;
periodic passage of the satellite forces this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:43.840
division to stay open, and separates the&nbsp;
rings which are situated on either side.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.800
This is Prometheus, and Pandora.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:08.560
Prometheus and Pandora are two shepherd&nbsp;
moons, which work together. Orbiting very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:14.080
close to each other, the two moons deflect&nbsp;
the trajectories of particles around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:25.840
and force them to be confined inside a very&nbsp;
narrow ring, barely a hundred kilometers wide.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:39.840
As we move away from Saturn and its rings,&nbsp;
we encounter a wide variety of moons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.080
each of them with unique characteristics.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.040
240,000 km from Saturn lies Enceladus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:09.840
Smaller than our Moon, Enceladus is however one&nbsp;
of Saturn's largest satellites. This moon exhibits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:22.160
strange "plumes", geyser-like formations which&nbsp;
rise several hundred kilometers above its surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:31.520
Composed of particles of ice, gas, and dust,&nbsp;
these jets of matter emerge from cryovolcanoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:49.840
scattered across the surface of the satellite.&nbsp;
They bear witness to an intense internal activity.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.320
On its surface, Enceladus is covered with ice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:04.080
riddled with impact craters, and&nbsp;
notched with cracks and volcanic faults.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:14.560
This moon remains constantly in a frozen state,&nbsp;
at an average temperature of negative 200°C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:28.000
Despite this very low surface temperature, the&nbsp;
geological activity of Enceladus betrays the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:36.640
existence of an ocean of liquid water under the&nbsp;
ice, as well as the presence of organic compounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:45.840
Two ingredients essential for&nbsp;
the development of life...

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.840
We are now more than a million&nbsp;
kilometers away from Saturn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:08.160
It is at this distance that its&nbsp;
largest satellite orbits. Titan.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.520
Titan is home to a completely&nbsp;
different world from Enceladus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.880
Surrounded by a very dense atmosphere,&nbsp;
consisting mainly of nitrogen, we find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:32.560
here a very rich climate, with clouds, rains…&nbsp;
of methane, and even seasons, like on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.840
This thick cloud layer hides a&nbsp;
surface with impressive variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.520
not entirely unlike some of&nbsp;
the landscapes found on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:57.760
Crossed by rivers and lakes of methane, Titan&nbsp;
is also speckled with volcanoes, mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.440
and sand dunes.&nbsp;

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:10.320
Although frozen, given its distance from the&nbsp;
Sun, Titan surprisingly recalls the first moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:18.960
of our planet. The chemical composition of its&nbsp;
atmosphere contains complex organic molecules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:37.840
which might remind us of the prebiotic&nbsp;
conditions... at the origin of life on Earth.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.400
Visible from the Earth, it has been&nbsp;
observed since prehistoric times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.440
Saturn has always fascinated astronomers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:11.440
Its gigantic size and majestic rings make it a&nbsp;
marvel of the solar system. Better known today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:19.840
than ever before, it still holds many&nbsp;
mysteries, which have yet to be discovered.

